1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,070
Radio message: "Theo, Heinrich here. Have shot down two bombers."

2
00:00:03,120 --> 00:00:05,320
"Out of ammunition. I'm ramming now."

3
00:00:05,470 --> 00:00:08,320
"Goodbye, see you in Valhalla."

4
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:15,420
Those are said to have been Heinrich Ehrler's last words, radioed in April 1945.

5
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:20,150
Ehrler was one of the Luftwaffe's most successful fighter pilots.

6
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:24,553
With Jagdgeschwader 5 he flew in the far north, over Lapland, the

7
00:00:24,553 --> 00:00:29,640
Barents Sea and the Murmansk front, in snowstorms and polar night.

8
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:33,174
He was credited with almost 200 aerial victories, wore the

9
00:00:33,174 --> 00:00:37,570
Knight's Cross with Oak Leaves and became commander of his wing.

10
00:00:37,620 --> 00:00:42,480
But on 12 November 1944 everything changed.

11
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:47,180
British Lancaster bombers attacked the battleship Tirpitz off Tromsø.

12
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:50,673
Their Tallboy bombs, each over five tons, hit; the

13
00:00:50,673 --> 00:00:54,780
Tirpitz capsized and about a thousand sailors died.

14
00:00:54,940 --> 00:00:58,390
The fighters from nearby Bardufoss came too late.

15
00:00:58,440 --> 00:01:03,320
Warnings arrived late or not at all, communications failed.

16
00:01:03,440 --> 00:01:08,300
The leadership needed someone to blame, and they found him in Ehrler.

17
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:13,480
A court-martial sentenced him to three years in prison and took away his command.

18
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:17,200
The sentence was suspended so that he could fly again.

19
00:01:17,460 --> 00:01:22,317
In February 1945 he joined Jagdgeschwader 7, the world's

20
00:01:22,317 --> 00:01:27,860
first jet fighter unit, flying the Messerschmitt Me 262.

21
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:33,930
In April 1945 he took off against a large American bomber formation.

22
00:01:33,980 --> 00:01:37,090
According to the reports he shot down two bombers.

23
00:01:37,140 --> 00:01:39,580
Then his ammunition was gone.

24
00:01:39,660 --> 00:01:43,960
Whether he really rammed the third bomber is still disputed today.

25
00:01:44,020 --> 00:01:45,330
Only this is certain:

26
00:01:45,380 --> 00:01:48,260
His aircraft crashed near Stendal.

27
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:51,750
Heinrich Ehrler was 27 years old.

28
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:55,600
A little over a month later the war in Europe was over.
